\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The symbol map is an isomorphism}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{\gamma} be the homomorphism defined by the operators of
\ref{fdip-00QA}. The
symbol #{u\mapsto u\cdot 1} is an isomorphism of modules
#{\operatorname{Cl}(V,q_b)\to\bigwedge V}. In particular
#{\gamma} is injective. For the standard dot product this
removes the open injectivity sentence of \ref{fdip-0092}.}

\proof{
\p{The ordered monomials in a basis of #{V} span the Clifford
algebra. A square #{v^2} is the scalar #{q_b(v)}, and the
anticommutator replaces a transposed pair by
#{b(u,v)+b(v,u)} minus the swapped word. Induction on length
gives the spanning. Apply an ordered
monomial in the represented basis vectors to #{1}. Expanding
each factor as wedge plus contraction, every summand that uses a
contraction has degree less than #{r}, because a contraction
lowers degree and the contraction of #{1} is #{0}. The remaining
summand is the wedge #{e_{i_1}\wedge\cdots\wedge e_{i_r}}. Ordered by
increasing degree, the symbol matrix is triangular with
diagonal units. It is therefore an isomorphism. The symbol
factors through #{\gamma}, so #{\gamma} is injective.}

\p{The operators #{e_i\wedge+e_i\cdot} of
\ref{fdip-0092} are the case #{b(e_i,e_j)=\delta_{ij}}. The
symbol isomorphism applies to that case.}
}
